---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Retirement401kCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/Retirement401kCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="401k-calculator">
  <Retirement401kCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the 401(k) calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The 401(k) calculator adds your contributions and the employer match each month and grows the
      balance at a monthly rate. Your yearly contribution is <code>C = min(salary × c, limit)</code>, where
      <code>c</code> is the share of salary you put in. The employer match is
      <code>E = salary × min(c, cap) × match</code>: the employer pays the match rate on the part of your
      contribution up to the cap.
    </p>
    <p>
      The monthly rate is <code>i = (1 + R)^(1/12) − 1</code>, where <code>R</code> is the yearly return,
      so twelve months give exactly <code>R</code>. Each month the balance becomes
      <code>B × (1 + i) + (C + E) ÷ 12</code>. Your salary rises by the salary growth rate each year, and
      both contributions rise with it.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      At age 30 with 25,000 saved, a 70,000 salary, a 6% contribution and a match of 50% up to 6% of
      salary, you put in 4,200 in the first year and the employer adds 2,100. With 2% salary growth and a
      6% return, the balance at 65 is 1,112,097. Of that, 209,977 is your money, 104,988 is the employer's
      and 772,132 is growth. The monthly rate is 0.4868%.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The return is the same every year, and fees, inflation and the tax on withdrawals are left out. The
      contribution limit stays the same each year; the IRS raises it with inflation, and people aged 50 or
      older can add catch-up contributions. Employer money can need some years of service before it is
      yours.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
